#What are Containers?

Lightweight packages containing an app + everything it needs to run (code, libraries, dependencies)
Isolated from other apps but share the same OS kernel
Portable: run the same way anywhere (laptop, server, cloud)

#What is Docker?

Most popular tool for creating and running containers
Key components: Docker Engine (runs containers), Images (blueprints), Dockerfiles (build scripts)

#Why Important for Security?
Advantages:

Isolation - Apps separated; if one is hacked, others stay safe
Quick updates - Rebuild and redeploy instead of patching servers
Consistency - Same environment everywhere = fewer hidden bugs
Disposable - Delete and recreate easily, harder for attackers to persist

#Risks to Watch:

Bad images - Downloading containers with malware/vulnerabilities
Misconfigurations - Running with too many privileges (especially as root)
Container escape - Attacker breaks out to attack the host system
Exposed secrets - Passwords/keys accidentally left in images
Supply chain - Compromised dependencies

#Security Best Practices:

Scan images for vulnerabilities before deploying
Use minimal base images (less attack surface)
Never run containers as root
Keep secrets out of images (use secret management tools)
Monitor container runtime behavior

